Group Travel Product Requirements
iPhone-first prototype · Three-hour hackathon · $9 Glasser budget
1. Product overview
A shared travel planner where friends contribute Instagram Reels, evaluate the places together, and turn their suggestions into an editable trip organized by day.
The app extracts places from shared videos, researches practical details, and explains why each place might suit the group. Friends express interest or flag concerns about price, distance, or effort. The app then proposes a rough plan using those suggestions and preferences.
The prototype will support approximately 20–30 public Instagram Reels.
2. Why we are building this
Friends already share travel ideas. The difficult part comes afterward:
Identifying the places shown in each video.
Checking whether they are worth visiting.
Finding their location and approximate cost.
Understanding who actually wants to go.
Arranging activities into a realistic trip.
That work often falls to one organizer. Other friends contribute ideas without considering how they fit together, then raise concerns after someone has assembled the plan.
We want to reduce that research and coordination work while keeping everyone involved. The original video, contributor, preferences, and concerns should remain connected to each suggestion as it moves into the plan.
3. Ideal customer profile
Our initial audience is groups of 3–8 college friends or young adults who:
Have chosen a destination.
Discover travel ideas on Instagram.
Have different budgets, interests, and preferred activity levels.
Want help planning without committing to a tightly scheduled itinerary.
This is an initial customer hypothesis to validate.
Primary user: The organizer
The organizer creates the trip, collects suggestions, researches options, and tries to assemble a plan that works for the group.
They need less manual research and a clear view of people’s preferences and concerns.
Secondary user: The contributor
The contributor sends interesting places but may not want to organize the trip.
They need a quick way to save ideas, show interest, and raise a concern without doing additional research.
Low-engagement user
Some friends contribute little until they disagree with a suggestion.
The app should make it easy for them to state preferences early or flag a specific concern later. Participation should take a few taps.
4. Expected benefits
User
Expected benefit
Organizer
Less time reconstructing videos, researching places, and chasing responses.
Contributor
Their suggestions stay visible and are considered when planning.
Less involved friend
A simple way to express preferences or objections.
Whole group
A shared plan that reflects broad interest and makes tradeoffs visible.

These are intended benefits. We have not measured them yet.
5. Existing products and our opportunity
Social imports, shared maps, and collaborative itineraries already exist. Our opportunity is to make the group’s decision process easier.
Comparison note: The table reflects published product descriptions, not hands-on testing. Where a feature was not established in the reviewed material, it is labeled “not verified.” That does not prove the competitor lacks it.
Alternative
What it already does
What our proposed experience adds
What is missing or not verified
Wanderlog
Itinerary planning, maps, collaboration, reservations, and imports from links or Reels.
Interest and concern signals beside each place, recommendations that explain group fit, and loose day planning informed by those signals.
We have not verified whether it combines individual preferences, structured price/distance/effort concerns, and explanations of how those concerns affect the proposed plan.
Mindtrip
Extracts details from social content for guides and supports personalized trip planning and co-planning.
A focused friend-group workflow that preserves who suggested each place, who is interested, and what concerns remain unresolved.
We have not verified an equivalent workflow connecting structured member concerns directly to editable day suggestions.
Group chats
Familiar, low-effort sharing and discussion.
Automatically researched place cards, duplicate merging, visible reactions, and a persistent day-by-day plan.
Ordinary chat messages do not automatically become structured place records or an editable itinerary. Decisions require manual tracking or additional tools.
Shared notes or spreadsheets
Flexible lists, collaborative editing, and manually organized plans.
Video extraction, place matching, researched briefs, and recommendations based on group preferences.
These capabilities generally require manual work or separately configured integrations.

Wanderlog’s developer listing explicitly advertises importing links and sharing Reels to create itineraries. Mindtrip describes social-post extraction and co-planning. We should therefore avoid pitching Reel import alone as our differentiator. apps.apple.com
Our differentiation hypothesis
The strongest opportunity is connecting these pieces in one simple workflow:
A friend’s suggestion → researched place → group interest and concerns → explained day assignment.
For example:
“Four people are interested in this waterfall. It fits the shared budget, but one person flagged the steep walk. We placed it on a lighter day and kept the concern visible.”
We need to test whether this helps groups make decisions more easily than their current tools.
6. Product principles
Save immediately
Store the shared link before researching it. People should be able to return to scrolling without waiting for processing.
Explain recommendations
Use short, specific reasons based on retrieved information and group preferences. Avoid unsupported numerical match scores.
Keep important concerns visible
A positive recommendation does not mean everyone agrees. Show individual concerns alongside group interest.
Preserve flexibility
Use loose day buckets and an Unassigned section. Support both spontaneous and organized groups.
Make correction easy
People should be able to fix a place match, add a missed suggestion, or move an activity without restarting the process.
7. User journey
Step 1: Create a trip
The organizer enters:
Destination.
Travel dates.
Accommodation or base area.
Transport mode.
Approximate per-person activity budget.
Shared interests.
Preferred pace or activity level.
Dates and accommodation can be marked unknown. Missing information should reduce the specificity of recommendations rather than block setup.
Step 2: Invite friends
Friends join through a private invite link and add:
Display name.
A few interests.
Budget preference.
Preferred activity level.
Optional firm limits or accessibility needs.
Individual preferences remain separate from the shared trip profile. The app should explain which preferences are visible to other members.
Step 3: Save a Reel from an iPhone
The intended experience is:
Instagram Share → trip-saving action → choose trip → Save.
For the prototype, test an installed iOS Shortcut that receives the shared URL and submits it to our backend.
The fallback is:
Copy Link → open the mobile website → paste → Save.
A native Share Extension is a later implementation. A mobile website alone should not be assumed to appear as an iPhone share target. developer.apple.com
Once saved, show:
“Saved. Researching your suggestions.”
The user can leave immediately.
Step 4: Extract and research places
If a Reel contains five identifiable places, automatically create five separate suggestions.
If the same place has already been added, combine the suggestions while preserving all contributors and original links.
Ambiguous matches appear as Needs confirmation and stay out of automatic planning until corrected.
Step 5: Review the place cards
Each card includes:
Place name.
Brief description.
What makes it distinctive.
Location.
Available approximate cost, including currency and whether it is per person.
Original Reel and contributor.
One or two meaningful caveats.
A short recommendation for the group.
Interest and concern signals.
An expanded view shows supporting sources and when the information was retrieved.
Step 6: React and flag concerns
Members can select:
Interested.
Pass.
Price concern.
Distance concern.
Effort concern.
Concerns can include an optional note.
No response remains separate from Pass. A concern stays visible for discussion and does not automatically veto the suggestion.
Step 7: Generate a rough plan
The app proposes:
Day 1.
Day 2.
Additional days as needed.
Unassigned ideas.
The plan favors broad group interest, groups nearby activities, and considers effort and known constraints.
Each day has a brief explanation of why those activities were grouped.
Step 8: Edit together
Members can:
Move an activity to another day.
Return an activity to Unassigned.
Remove an activity from the plan without deleting the saved idea.
Review the original video and concerns.
Changes are saved for everyone. Regeneration requires an explicit action and must not silently overwrite edits.
8. Data sources and visual extraction
Supported sources
Source
Purpose
Prototype scope
Public Instagram Reels
Original inspiration, caption, available media, and optional transcript.
Primary input.
Place search through Glasser
Resolve names to real locations and retrieve available addresses, coordinates, and ratings.
Core enrichment.
Official business or attraction websites
Verify available prices, booking requirements, and practical details.
Targeted research.
Review endpoints
Support balanced pros and caveats using actual review text.
Include only if coverage is validated.
TikTok and YouTube Shorts
Additional inspiration sources.
Deferred.

How visually heavy Reels are handled
Glasser retrieves the media. A separate multimodal model interprets sampled frames and available text.
Where information appears
Extraction method
Post caption
Extract names from retrieved text.
Spoken narration
Use an available transcript.
On-screen labels
Sample video frames and use a vision model to read them.
Unnamed scenery
Save the inspiration and request clarification when necessary.

The verified Glasser endpoints provide Reel information, transcripts, and media downloads. They do not establish built-in visual identification of places. The application must perform that processing. glasser.ai
Frame sampling may miss text that appears briefly. Users must be able to add a missed place or correct an extraction.
9. System architecture
Use one mobile web frontend, one backend, and one shared database.
Component
Responsibility
iPhone capture
Shortcut or paste-link page sends the URL and trip reference.
Mobile web app
Trip setup, Ideas and Plan views, preferences, reactions, corrections, and day edits.
Backend
Checks access, stores imports, runs research, calls Glasser and the model, and tracks spend.
Database
Stores trips, members, imports, places, evidence, reactions, concerns, and day assignments.
Background processing
Processes saved links and updates their status.
Glasser
Retrieves approved social, place, and supporting website data.
Multimodal model
Extracts information from frames and text, writes briefs, and proposes day assignments.

Processing one Reel
Save and deduplicate. Normalize the URL, store the contributor, and check whether the Reel was already processed.
Retrieve content. Use Glasser to fetch the Reel and downloadable media.
Extract candidates. Process the caption, sampled frames, and transcript when needed.
Match places. Search candidate names with destination context and check the location.
Research details. Retrieve supporting information for the matched places.
Create cards. Save the brief, evidence, recommendation, and source links.
Update status. Mark the import Ready, Partially ready, Needs confirmation, or Failed.
Successful places should remain available if another place in the same Reel fails.
Main data records
Record
Key information
Trip
Destination, dates, base areas, transport, shared preferences.
Member
Stable identity, trip membership, personal preferences.
Import
Original URL, contributor, processing status, errors.
Place
Matched identity, location, brief, practical details, evidence.
Reaction
Member, place, Interested or Pass.
Concern
Member, place, concern type, optional note.
Day assignment
Place, day, position, explanation.

Access and storage
Keep provider keys on the backend. Check trip membership on every request.
Use revocable invite links and a session for each joined member. A display name alone is not authentication.
Delete temporary media after processing and follow provider restrictions on retention and reuse.
10. How recommendations and planning work
Place recommendations
Each recommendation should explain:
Why the experience matches the group’s interests.
Whether available cost information fits the budget.
Relevant distance or effort considerations.
Important concerns or missing information.
Use plain labels such as Strong option, Worth discussing, or Needs more information, supported by a short explanation.
A recommendation must not imply unanimous agreement.
Rough plan generation
The app should:
Start with confirmed saved places.
Respect explicit firm limits.
Favor broad interest while keeping missing responses separate.
Group places by geographic area.
Account for available duration and effort information.
Leave room for downtime.
Keep additional suggestions in Unassigned.
Concern flags are discussion signals. They become hard exclusions only when someone explicitly identifies a firm limit.
Nearby coordinates support geographic grouping. They do not establish driving time or route practicality.
Validate generated plans
Before saving a generated plan, check that:
Every assigned place belongs to the trip.
Day numbers are valid.
Activities are not accidentally duplicated.
Unconfirmed places are excluded.
Existing edits are preserved or replacement is explicitly confirmed.
Opening hours, closures, and booking requirements should remain unverified unless supporting information was retrieved.
11. Reliability and cost controls
Reliability requirements
Test actual endpoint responses before building the parser.
Keep the original link when processing fails.
Preserve partial results.
Allow manual place entry and correction.
Never invent place names, prices, addresses, or review consensus.
Use actual review text before writing review-based pros and cons.
Show unknown information clearly.
Glasser’s verified Reel endpoints do not publish an output schema, so response handling must be based on real tests. glasser.ai
Efficiency requirements
Use a fixed set of approved endpoints.
Pin endpoint versions where supported.
Deduplicate links and places before enrichment.
Reuse research where provider terms permit.
Use timeouts and bounded retries.
Reuse idempotency keys for transport retries.
Make no Glasser calls for votes, concerns, or day edits.
Generate plans from stored research.
