12. Mobile experience and accessibility
Mobile layout
Use a single-column layout with two main views:
Ideas: Saved places, reactions, and concerns.
Plan: Day buckets and Unassigned ideas.
Keep an obvious Add action available. Put the essential information on each card and allow expansion for research details.
Interface accessibility
Use touch targets at least 44 by 44 points.
Support larger text without clipping controls.
Label controls for VoiceOver.
Show reactions and concerns with text, not color alone.
Provide text equivalents for video-derived suggestions.
Avoid autoplaying videos.
Offer list access to anything shown on a map.
Provide a “Move to day” control so dragging is optional.
Announce processing results without repeatedly interrupting reading.
Preserve saved links through network failures and provide retry actions.
Travel accessibility
Let members state mobility, dietary, or activity limitations.
Do not label a place wheelchair accessible or suitable for an allergy without supporting evidence. Missing information should stay visible so the group can verify it.
13. Prototype scope
Build
Shared trip creation and joining.
Shared and individual preferences.
iPhone link capture with a paste fallback.
Public Instagram Reel processing.
Separate place cards for identifiable suggestions.
Duplicate merging.
Short researched briefs and group recommendations.
Interest reactions and structured concerns.
Rough day-plan generation.
Manual day changes and Unassigned ideas.
Saved state across refreshes.
Defer
Native app distribution.
TikTok and YouTube support.
Booking and payments.
Expense splitting.
Hourly schedules.
Live route optimization.
Continuous automatic replanning.
Full group chat.
Push notifications.
If visual extraction proves unreliable, narrow support to captions and transcripts with manual place entry. Clearly describe that limitation.
14. Three-hour build plan
Time
Deliverable
0–30 minutes
Test five varied Reels through Glasser and the model. Test iPhone capture. Confirm a working extraction route.
30–100 minutes
Build trip setup, joining, link saving, research processing, and place cards.
100–145 minutes
Add reactions, concerns, rough-plan generation, and day controls.
145–180 minutes
Test the multi-user iPhone demo, fix failures, and check spend.

The first 30 minutes determine whether the intended visual workflow is feasible within the remaining time.
15. Acceptance criteria
Complete group workflow
A three-person group can:
Join the same trip.
Add preferences.
Share or paste a Reel.
Review extracted places.
Express interest and flag a concern.
Generate a rough plan.
Move an activity to another day.
See the saved change from another member’s device.
Data and reliability
Duplicate imports do not repeat enrichment.
Uncertain matches stay out of generated plans.
Original links and contributors remain attached.
Refreshing retains reactions and edits.
Partial failures preserve successful place cards.
Missing information is not fabricated.
Suggested technical targets
Recover at least 80% of explicitly named places across ten representative Reels.
Make no incorrect automatic place matches in that test.
Complete most imports within 90 seconds.
Keep total Glasser spending below $9.
These are proposed targets, not observed results. Record failures and avoid treating a small test as proof of general reliability.
16. Product validation
After the demo, ask:
Did the group spend less time researching shared videos?
Did members understand why places were recommended?
Were concerns raised early enough to influence planning?
Could the group edit the plan without depending entirely on one organizer?
Did the loose day structure work for both organized and spontaneous planning?
Compare the same planning task with the group’s current workflow. The product earns its place if people can reach a useful shared plan with less research and coordination.
17. Open questions before implementation
Does the iOS Shortcut receive a usable URL from Instagram’s actual share flow?
Can sampled frames reliably capture names in rapid-cut Reels?
How well do place searches handle ambiguous names and multi-location businesses?
Which sources provide usable review text and cost information?
What model-processing budget is available separately from Glasser?
Is the full workflow achievable in three hours, or should the demo narrow extraction support?
